# cFish.io WordPress Development Glossary

A quick reference guide to WordPress and web development terminology used in the cFish.io project.

## WordPress Core Concepts

### A

**Action Hook**  
Points in WordPress code where you can insert custom functions to be executed at specific times.

**Admin Bar**  
The black bar that appears at the top of the screen when logged in.

**Attachment**  
A file uploaded to the WordPress Media Library.

### B

**Backend**  
The administrative area of WordPress (wp-admin).

**Block**  
Content elements in the WordPress Block Editor (Gutenberg).

**Block Editor (Gutenberg)**  
The current WordPress content editor that uses blocks for content creation.

### C

**Child Theme**  
A theme that inherits functionality and styling from another theme (the parent theme).

**Constant**  
A value defined in PHP that cannot be changed during script execution.

**Custom Field**  
Extra information saved with a post or page (also known as meta data).

**Custom Post Type**  
Content types beyond the default posts and pages.

### D

**Dashboard**  
The main administration screen in WordPress.

**Database**  
Where WordPress stores all content and settings information.

**Deprecated**  
Code or functions that have been replaced with newer alternatives and will be removed in future versions.

### E

**Enqueue**  
The proper way to include CSS and JavaScript files in WordPress.

**Excerpt**  
A summary or snippet of a post.

### F

**Filter Hook**  
Allows you to modify data before it's used by WordPress.

**Frontend**  
The public-facing side of the website that visitors see.

**Functions.php**  
A theme file used to add features and modify WordPress behavior.

### G

**Global Variable**  
Variables in WordPress that are accessible throughout the code (e.g., `$post`, `$wpdb`).

### H

**Hook**  
Points in WordPress code where you can insert custom functionality (actions or filters).

**htaccess**  
A configuration file used on web servers running Apache.

### I

**Include**  
PHP function to include and execute another file.

**Index.php**  
The main template file in a WordPress theme.

### J

**Jetpack**  
A popular WordPress plugin suite by Automattic offering various functionality.

### L

**Loop**  
The code used to display posts in WordPress themes.

### M

**Meta Box**  
UI elements on the post editing screen for adding metadata.

**Multisite**  
A WordPress feature allowing multiple websites to share a single WordPress installation.

### P

**Parent Theme**  
A complete theme that can be used as a foundation for a child theme.

**Plugin**  
Software that extends WordPress functionality.

**Post**  
A content type in WordPress (blog posts, articles).

**Post Meta**  
Additional data stored with a post.

### Q

**Query**  
Request for specific information from the WordPress database.

**Query String**  
Part of a URL that contains data to be passed to web applications.

### R

**Responsive Design**  
Design approach making web pages render well on different devices/screen sizes.

**REST API**  
Interface allowing authorized external applications to interact with WordPress.

**Revision**  
Saved versions of a post or page.

### S

**Shortcode**  
Code in square brackets that adds functionality to posts and pages.

**Sidebar**  
Widget-ready areas in themes.

**Slug**  
URL-friendly version of a name, consisting of lowercase letters, numbers, and hyphens.

### T

**Taxonomy**  
Way to organize content in WordPress (categories, tags, etc.).

**Template**  
Files that control how WordPress content is displayed.

**Theme**  
Collection of files that determine the appearance of a WordPress site.

### U

**Upload Directory**  
Folder where media files are stored (typically /wp-content/uploads/).

### W

**Widget**  
Standalone blocks of content or functionality placed in widget areas.

**wp-config.php**  
Configuration file containing database connection details and other settings.

**WP_Query**  
Class used to create custom database queries.

## Development & Git Terms

### A

**Attribute**  
HTML code providing additional information about HTML elements.

### B

**Branch**  
A separate line of development in Git.

**Build**  
Process of preparing code for deployment.

### C

**Commit**  
Saving changes to a Git repository.

**CSS (Cascading Style Sheets)**  
Language used for styling HTML elements.

### D

**Dependency**  
External code that your project relies on.

**Development Environment**  
Local setup for building and testing code before deployment.

### F

**Feature Branch**  
Git branch created for developing a specific feature.

**Fork**  
Copy of a repository that allows experimentation without affecting the original project.

### G

**Git**  
Version control system for tracking changes in files.

**GitHub**  
Web-based hosting service for Git repositories.

### H

**HTML (HyperText Markup Language)**  
Standard markup language for creating web pages.

### J

**JavaScript**  
Programming language that enables interactive web pages.

**JSON (JavaScript Object Notation)**  
Lightweight data interchange format.

### M

**Merge**  
Combining changes from different branches in Git.

**Migration**  
Process of transferring data between systems or formats.

### P

**PHP**  
Server-side scripting language WordPress is built with.

**Production Environment**  
Live website accessible to the public.

**Pull Request**  
Proposed changes to a repository submitted for review.

### R

**Repository**  
Storage location for a project, containing files and revision history.

**Responsive Design**  
Approach to web design that makes pages render well on various devices.

### S

**Staging Environment**  
Testing environment that mimics production for final testing.

**SSH (Secure Shell)**  
Network protocol for secure data communication.

### V

**Version Control**  
System that records changes to files over time.

## WordPress.com Specific Terms

**WordPress.com**  
Hosted WordPress platform by Automattic.

**WordPress Studio**  
Local development environment for WordPress.com sites.

**Jetpack Backup**  
WordPress.com service providing backup and restore functionality.

**Plan**  
Subscription level on WordPress.com determining available features.

**Business Plan**  
WordPress.com plan that allows plugin installation and theme uploads.

**Custom CSS**  
WordPress.com feature allowing CSS customization without modifying theme files.